"Non-transitive Dice - Microsoft Research (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/non-transitive-dice/)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/non-transitive-dice/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 60\nL0: cite0†Image L1: # Non-transitive Dice\nL2: \nL3: Embracing Uncertainty\nL4: \nL5: Scientists have worked for decades to try to create intelligence in computers. Traditional approaches relied on hand-crafted solutions and had limited applicability. New artificial intelligence techniques developed at Microsoft and other institutions around the world are based on computers that can learn for themselves by analysing large sets of data.\nL6: The key to learning from examples is to recognise that real-world data is full of complexity, ambiguity, and uncertainty. Computers can be programmed to handle these challenges by using a branch of mathematics called probability theory. By embracing the mathematics of uncertainty, we are able to create machines that can learn from data and apply these to problems ranging from medical data analysis to the recognition of objects in images.\nL7: To learn more about Microsoft’s work in artificial intelligence, visit aka.ms/artificialintelligence\nL8: \nL9: About the dice\nL10: \nL11: First of all, let us examine the dice in detail. Here we have ‘flattened’ the dice to show the numbers on each of the faces.\nL12: cite1†Image: dice_illustration We see that the choice of numbers is unusual. But in all other respects these are normal, fair dice. If we pick two of the dice and roll them, we can see which comes up with the higher number. We will say that the die with the higher number is the winner. Notice that each of the numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 appears on only one of the dice, which means that when we roll one die against another, there can never be a draw.\nL13: First, suppose you roll the orange and yellow dice. Two thirds of the time the yellow die will come up with a higher number than the orange die. We say that the probability that the yellow die will win is two thirds. If we make a large number of such rolls and keep track of which die wins, then there is a very high chance that the yellow die will win more often than the orange die.\nL14: Likewise, two thirds of the time the blue die will win against the yellow die, and two thirds of the time the green die will win against the blue die. So, yellow beats orange, blue beats yellow, and green beats blue.\nL15: \nL16: It therefore seems as if the green die has the highest chance of winning and the orange die has the lowest. But now for the surprise: if you roll the green die with the orange die, then two thirds of the time it is the orange die that will win!\nL17: These are known as non-transitive dice. Transitive means that if A beats B and B beats C then A beats C. We see that these dice do not have this property. One way to visualise this is to arrange the dice in a circle, such that each die beats the previous one.\nL18: \nL19: This is rather like the game of “rock, paper, scissors” in which scissors beats paper, and rock beats scissors, but paper beats rock.\nL20: \nL21: A simple game\nL22: You can use this non-transitive property to play a game with a friend. Invite them to examine the dice and then to select any one of them. Without telling them the secret, you now select the next die in the sequence, and then you make, say, 9 rolls against your friend, and keep note of how many times each of you rolls the higher number. Over a sequence of 9 rolls it is very likely that you will roll a higher number more often than your friend.\nL23: \nL24: How the dice work\nL25: cite2†Image: dice_diagram-rgb Look first at the orange die, and notice that it has four copies of the number 2 and two copies of the number 6. Two-thirds of the time, when we roll the orange die it will give a 2, and one third of the time it will give a 6. Therefore, if we roll the orange die against the yellow die (which always gives a 3), the yellow die will, on average, win two-thirds of the time, and will lose one-third of the time.\nL26: You can repeat this game several times, each time allowing your friend to choose their die first.\nL27: Now look at the blue die, and notice that it has four copies of the number 4, and two copies of the number 0. When we roll it against the yellow die, it will therefore give a 4 two thirds of the time, in which case it wins, and a 0 one-third of the time, in which case it loses.\nL28: Now suppose we roll the green die against the blue die. The green die has three copies of the number 1 and three copies of the number 5. To work out the probability that the green die will win we first note that there is a probability of 1/2 that the green die will give a 5, in which case it is certain to win against the blue die. Likewise, there is a probability of 1/2 that the green die will give a 1, in which case there is a probability of 1/3 that it will win.\nL29: The overall probability that the green die will win is then given by multiplying the probabilities:\nL30: cite3†Image L31: \nL32: Finally, consider the probability of the orange die winning against the green die. There is a probability of 1/3 that the orange die will produce a 6, in which case it is certain that the orange die will win. There is similarly a probability of 2/3 that the orange die will produce a 2 in which case there is a 1/2 chance that the orange die will win. The overall probability of the orange die winning is again obtained by multiplying the probabilities:\nL33: \nL34: cite4†Image L35: \nL36: Further information\nL37: Warren Buffett once challenged Bill Gates to a game of dice. “Buffett suggested that each of them choose one of the dice, then discard the other two. They would bet on who would roll the higher number most often. Buffett offered to let Gates pick his die first. This suggestion instantly aroused Gates’ curiosity. He asked to examine the dice, after which he demanded that Buffett choose first.” Buffett was using a set of non-transitive dice!\nL38: \nL39: Opens in a new tab\nL40: \nL41: Follow us:\nL42:   * cite5†Follow on X†x.com L43:   * cite6†Like on Facebook†www.facebook.com L44:   * cite7†Follow on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L45:   * cite8†Subscribe on Youtube†www.youtube.com L46:   * cite9†Follow on Instagram†www.instagram.com L47:   * cite10†Subscribe to our RSS feed L48: \nL49: Share this page:\nL50: \nL51:   * cite11†Share on X†x.com L52:   * cite12†Share on Facebook†www.facebook.com L53:   * cite13†Share on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L54:   * cite14†Share on Reddit†www.reddit.com L55: cite15†Surface Laptop Ultra cite16†Surface Pro cite17†Surface Laptop cite18†Surface RTX Spark Dev Box cite19†Explore Copilot cite20†Explore Microsoft products cite21†Windows 11 apps cite22†Account profile†account.microsoft.com cite23†Download Center cite24†Microsoft Store support†go.microsoft.com cite25†Returns cite26†Order tracking cite27†Certified Refurbished cite28†Why buy from Microsoft Store cite29†Flexible Payments cite30†Microsoft in education cite31†Devices for education cite32†Microsoft Teams for Education cite33†Microsoft 365 Education cite34†How to buy for your school cite35†Educator training and development†education.microsoft.com cite36†Deals for students and parents cite37†AI for education L56: cite38†Microsoft AI cite39†Microsoft Security cite40†Dynamics 365 cite41†Microsoft 365 cite42†Microsoft Power Platform cite43†Microsoft Teams cite44†Microsoft Copilot cite45†Small Business cite46†Azure†azure.microsoft.com cite47†Microsoft Developer†developer.microsoft.com cite48†Microsoft Learn†learn.microsoft.com cite49†Support for AI marketplace apps cite50†Microsoft Tech Community†techcommunity.microsoft.com cite51†Microsoft Marketplace†marketplace.microsoft.com cite52†Software companies cite53†Visual Studio†visualstudio.microsoft.com cite54†Careers†careers.microsoft.com cite55†About Microsoft cite56†Company news†news.microsoft.com cite57†Privacy at Microsoft cite58†Investors cite59†Diversity and inclusion cite60†Accessibility cite61†Sustainability L57: cite62†Your Privacy Choices†aka.ms cite62†Your Privacy Choices†aka.ms L58: \nL59: cite63†Consumer Health Privacy†go.microsoft.com cite64†Sitemap cite65†Contact Microsoft†support.microsoft.com cite66†Privacy†go.microsoft.com cite67†Manage cookies cite68†Terms of use†go.microsoft.com cite69†Trademarks†go.microsoft.com cite70†Safety & eco†go.microsoft.com cite71†Recycling cite72†About our ads†choice.microsoft.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNontransitive and Balanced Sets of Dice - Harvard Math (https://www.math.harvard.edu/event/nontransitive-and-balanced-sets-of-dice/)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.math.harvard.edu/event/nontransitive-and-balanced-sets-of-dice/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 56\nL0:   * cite0†Home L1:   * cite1†About L2:   * cite2†People L3:   * cite3†Events L4:   * cite4†Community†sites.harvard.edu L5:   * cite5†Undergraduate L6:   * cite6†Graduate L7:   * cite7†Jobs L8:   * cite8†Resources L9: \nL10: cite9†Support Harvard Math L11: \nL12: [Input: Search …] [Input]\nL13: # Nontransitive and Balanced Sets of Dice\nL14: \nL15: MATH TABLE\nL16: \nL17: When: November 20, 2024\nL18: \nL19: 4:30 pm - 5:30 pm\nL20: \nL21: Where: Science Center 507\nL22: \nL23: Address: 1 Oxford Street, Cambridge, MA 02138, United States\nL24: \nL25: Speaker: Josh Rooney - Harvard\nL26: Let’s recall the game “rock-paper-scissors.” Although rock beats scissors and scissors beats paper, rock does not beat paper. We call this property “nontransitivity.” Does this same idea translate to dice? In 1970, Martin Gardner popularized a set of 4 dice (A,B,C,D) “Efron dice” in which P(B>A)= P(C>B) = P(D>C) = P(A>D) = 2/3.\nL27: Since these probabilities are greater than 1/2, this set of dice is nontransitive, and since the probabilities are equal, we will call the set “balanced.” The dominance probability, 2/3 in this case, that is possible for such a set of dice is dependent on the number of dice in the set. In this talk, we will fully classify all such probabilities for a set of n nontransitive and balanced dice. This talk will aim to be accessible to all undergraduates.\nL28:   *     * cite10†Announcements L29:     * cite11†Recent News L30: \nL31: * * *\nL32: \nL33:   * cite12†Announcements Archive L34:   * cite13†Conference Archive L35:   * cite14†Events Archive L36:   * cite15†News Archive L37: \nL38: Harvard University\nL39: Department of Mathematics\nL40: Science Center Room 325\nL41: 1 Oxford Street\nL42: Cambridge, MA 02138\nL43: USA\nL44: \nL45: Tel: (617) 495-2171 Fax: (617) 495-5132\nL46: \nL47: Department Main Office Contact (mainoffice@math.harvard.edu)\nL48: cite16†Digital Accessibility†accessibility.huit.harvard.edu L49: \nL50: cite17†Legacy Department of Mathematics Website†legacy-www.math.harvard.edu L51: ###### Associated Center\nL52: \nL53: cite18†Center of Mathematical Sciences and Applications†cmsa.fas.harvard.edu L54: \nL55: © 2026 The President and Fellows of Harvard College. Designed by cite19†SmartSites†www.smartsites.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEfron's Dice -- from Wolfram MathWorld (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EfronsDice.html)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EfronsDice.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 66\nL0: cite0†Image TOPICS\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search] cite1†Image: Search cite2†Image: Close L3: \nL4: cite3†Image L5: \nL6: [Input: Search] cite1†Image: Search L7: \nL8: cite4†Algebra cite5†Applied Mathematics cite6†Calculus and Analysis cite7†Discrete Mathematics cite8†Foundations of Mathematics cite9†Geometry cite10†History and Terminology cite11†Number Theory cite12†Probability and Statistics cite13†Recreational Mathematics cite14†Topology cite15†Alphabetical Index cite16†New in MathWorld L9: # Efron's Dice\nL10: \nL11: * * *\nL12: \nL13: cite17†Download Wolfram Notebook L14: \nL15: Efron's dice are set of four nontransitive cite18†dice such that the probabilities of A winning against B, B against C, C against D, and D against A are all the same.\nL16: \nL17: cite19†Image: EfronsDice L18: \nL19: The images above depict two different sets of Efron's dice having 2:1 odds for winning pairs.\nL20: \nL21: cite20†Image: EfronsDiceModified L22: Another set of dice in which ties may occur (in which case the cite18†dice are rolled again) and which gives cite21†odds of 11:6 for winning pairs is illustrated above.\nL23: \nL24: * * *\nL25: ## See also\nL26: \nL27: cite18†Dice , cite22†Sicherman Dice L28: \nL29: ## Explore with Wolfram|Alpha\nL30: \nL31: cite23†Image: WolframAlpha L32: \nL33: [Input: Solve your math problems and get step-by-step solutions]\nL34: \nL35: More things to try:\nL36: \nL37:   * cite24†boxcars†www.wolframalpha.com L38:   * cite25†20%†www.wolframalpha.com L39:   * cite26†gamma(x) integral representation†www.wolframalpha.com L40: ## References\nL41: \nL42: Gardner, M. \"Mathematical Games: The Paradox of the Nontransitive Dice and the Elusive Principle of Indifference.\" Sci. Amer. 223, 110-114, Dec. 1970.Honsberger, R. \"Some Surprises in Probability.\" Ch. 5 in cite27†Mathematical Plums†www.amazon.com (Ed. R. Honsberger). Washington, DC: Math. Assoc. Amer., pp. 94-97, 1979.\nL43: \nL44: ## Referenced on Wolfram|Alpha\nL45: \nL46: cite28†Efron's Dice†www.wolframalpha.com L47: ## Cite this as:\nL48: \nL49: cite29†Weisstein, Eric W. \"Efron's Dice.\" From cite30†MathWorld --A Wolfram Resource. cite31†https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EfronsDice.html L50: ## Subject classifications\nL51: \nL52:   * cite32†About MathWorld L53:   * cite33†MathWorld Classroom L54:   * cite34†Contribute L55:   * cite35†MathWorld Book†www.amazon.com L56:   * cite36†wolfram.com†www.wolfram.com L57: \nL58:   * cite16†15,337 Entries L59:   * cite16†Last Updated: Fri Oct 2 2026 L60:   * cite36†©1999–2026 Wolfram Research, Inc.†www.wolfram.com L61:   * cite37†Terms of Use†www.wolfram.com L62:   *   * cite36†wolfram.com†www.wolfram.com L63:   * cite38†Wolfram for Education†www.wolfram.com L64:   * Created, developed and nurtured by Eric Weisstein at Wolfram Research\nL65: Created, developed and nurtured by Eric Weisstein at Wolfram Research\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1610.08595)\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1610.08595\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 526\nL0@P0: arXiv:1610.08595v1 [math.CO] 27 Oct 2016\nL1@P0: A Direct Construction of Non-Transitive Dice\nL2@P0: Sets\nL3@P0: Levi Angel and Matt Davis∗\nL4@P0: Muskingum University\nL5@P0: October 28, 2016\nL6@P0: Abstract\nL7@P0: In this paper, we give a direct construction for a set of dice realizing any\nL8@P0: given tournament T . The construction for a tournament with n vertices\nL9@P0: requires a number of sides on the order of n, which is the best general\nL10@P0: construction to date. Our construction relies only on a standard theorem\nL11@P0: from graph theory.\nL12@P0: 1 Introduction\nL13@P0: Non-transitive dice have been a object of wide interest since Martin Gardner\nL14@P0: introduced some work (now known as Efron Dice) of Bradley Efron to the\nL15@P0: general public in [5]. For a set of at least 3 dice {X1, X2, X3, . . .}, with faces\nL16@P0: labeled in a nonstandard way, we define the relation \u0017 by declaring that Xi \u0017 Xj\nL17@P0: exactly if, when the dice are rolled, the probability that Xi rolls a higher number\nL18@P0: than Xj is greater than 1/2. Paradoxically, it is entirely possible to put numbers\nL19@P0: on the dice in order to make the relation \u0017 non-transitive. The most basic\nL20@P0: example of such a set of dice is the following set of three 3-sided dice:\nL21@P0: Die: Faces:\nL22@P0: X1 1,5,9\nL23@P0: X2 3,4,8\nL24@P0: X3 2,6,7\nL25@P0: (1)\nL26@P0: One can easily check that X1 \u0017 X2, X2 \u0017 X3, and X3 \u0017 X1, where the\nL27@P0: stronger die in each pair has probability 5/9 of winning. Note that we could\nL28@P0: widen our focus and examine non-transitive sets of general random variables.\nL29@P0: (See for example [8] and [9], which seem to predate any notion of non-transitive\nL30@P0: dice specifically). However, even focusing on dice, there are many open ques\u0002tions.\nL31@P0: Our aim in this paper is to consider larger sets of dice with arbitrary relations\nL32@P0: between them. A tournament on n vertices is a directed realization of the\nL33@P0: complete graph Kn. In other words, it is a directed graph on the vertices\nL34@P0: {1, 2, . . . n}, where for any pair of vertices i and j, either there is an edge from\nL35@P0: i to j or from j to i, but not both. We can interpret this as a definition of a\nL36@P0: ∗mattd@muskingum.edu\nL37@P0-1: 1relation on a set of dice - we say that a set of dice realizes a tournament T if\nL38@P1: Xi \u0017 Xj if and only if there is an edge from i to j in T . So the set of dice in\nL39@P1: (1) realizes the tournament in Figure 1.\nL40@P1: •\nL41@P1: • •\nL42@P1: 1\nL43@P1: 3 2\nL44@P1: .\nL45@P1: .\nL46@P1: ...\nL47@P1: ....\nL48@P1: ......\nL49@P1: ...\nL50@P1: .............. ................................................... ...................................................\nL51@P1: .............\nL52@P1: ...........................\nL53@P1: .\nL54@P1: ..\nL55@P1: ..\nL56@P1: ...\nL57@P1: .....\nL58@P1: ......\nL59@P1: Figure 1: A non-transitive tournament on 3 vertices\nL60@P1: A natural problem to solve is, given an arbitrary tournament T , to construct\nL61@P1: a set of dice which realize T . There are many examples of general constructions\nL62@P1: that can be used to solve this problem. But going further, can we find a set of\nL63@P1: dice with a relatively small number of sides that realizes T ? The most efficient\nL64@P1: general construction appears to be that of Bednay and Boz´oki ([2], Construction\nL65@P1: 5 and Corollary 7), which shows that any tournament with n vertices can be\nL66@P1: realized with a set of dice with ⌈\nL67@P1: 6\nL68@P1: 5\nL69@P1: n⌉ sides. In this paper, we give a construction\nL70@P1: which allows us to realize any tournament on n vertices with dice with at most\nL71@P1: n + 1 sides.\nL72@P1: 2 Preliminaries\nL73@P1: Our construction relies on a well-known construction from graph theory, which\nL74@P1: we recall here. The edge set of the (undirected) complete graph on the vertices\nL75@P1: {1, 2, . . .} is the set of all unordered pairs chosen from that set: {{1, 2}, {1, 3}, . . .{n−\nL76@P1: 1, n}}.\nL77@P1: Theorem 1. Let E = {{1, 2}, {1, 3}, . . .{n − 1, n}}. If n is even, there is a\nL78@P1: partition of E into n − 1 sets of size n/2, where no two pairs within a single set\nL79@P1: share an element. If n is odd, there is a partition of E into n sets of size n−1\nL80@P1: 2\nL81@P1: where no two pairs within a single set share an element.\nL82@P1: In the language of graph theory, this theorem establishes the edge chromatic\nL83@P1: number of Kn. As such, in the following we will refer to the pairs as edges and\nL84@P1: the elements of those pairs as vertices.\nL85@P1: We do not give a full proof of the theorem, since we only need the statement\nL86@P1: of the standard construction. In the odd case, let n = 2k + 1. Then, for i\nL87@P1: from 1 to n, we let Yi be the set containing the edges {i − 1, i + 1}, {i − 2, i +\nL88@P1: 2}, . . . {i − k, i + k}. We reduce the entries of these pairs mod n if needed, so\nL89@P1: that all entries are in the range from 1 to n. The standard proof of this theorem\nL90@P1: (see, for example [1]) shows that the set Yi form a partition of the edges of Kn,\nL91@P1: where a vertex j appears in exactly one edge in every set Yi except Yj . To be\nL92@P1: able to refer to specific pairs later, we let Yij = {i + j, i − j} for j from 1 to k.\nL93@P1: As an example for the next section, note that for n = 7, this algorithm creates\nL94@P1: the sets in Figure 2.\nL95@P1: In the even case, we let n = 2k and define the ith set of our partition, Yi, to\nL96@P1: contain the edges {i, n}, {i − 1, i + 1}, {i − 2, i + 2}, . . . {i − (k − 1), i + (k − 1)},\nL97@P1: where the entries besides n are reduced mod n − 1 to fall in the range between\nL98@P1: 1 and n − 1. (We will not mention this reduction later, but it is always in effect\nL99@P1-2: 2Set: Pairs:\nL100@P2: Y1 {2,7},{3,6},{4,5}\nL101@P2: Y2 {1,3},{4,7},{5,6}\nL102@P2: Y3 {2,4},{1,5},{6,7}\nL103@P2: Y4 {3,5},{2,6},{1,7}\nL104@P2: Y5 {4,6},{3,7},{1,2}\nL105@P2: Y6 {5,7},{1,4},{2,3}\nL106@P2: Y7 {1,6},{2,5},{3,4}\nL107@P2: Figure 2: The construction for n = 7\nL108@P2: when discussing these pairs.) In the case where n = 6, this creates the partition\nL109@P2: in Figure 3.\nL110@P2: Set: Pairs:\nL111@P2: Y1 {2,5},{1,6},{3,4}\nL112@P2: Y2 {1,3},{2,6},{4,5}\nL113@P2: Y3 {2,4},{3,6},{1,5}\nL114@P2: Y4 {3,5},{4,6},{1,2}\nL115@P2: Y5 {1,4},{5,6},{2,3}\nL116@P2: Figure 3: The construction for n = 6\nL117@P2: As above, the standard proof of the theorem shows that each edge appears\nL118@P2: once in this table, and each vertex appears once in each row of this table. An\nL119@P2: additional property of these sets that we will need in the even case is that for\nL120@P2: each j from 1 to k − 1, each vertex other than n appears exactly twice in sets of\nL121@P2: the form {i+j, i−j}. To see this, note that the two vertices in {i+j, i−j} have\nL122@P2: a difference of exactly 2j mod n−1. Thus for a vertex m, the sets containing m\nL123@P2: are exactly {m, m+ 2j} and {m, m−2j}. These sets are distinct since m cannot\nL124@P2: be congruent to m ± 2j mod n − 1 since 2j < n − 1, and if m + 2j ≡ m − 2j\nL125@P2: mod n − 1, then 4j ≡ 0 mod n − 1, and since n − 1 is odd, this would mean\nL126@P2: j ≡ 0 mod n − 1.\nL127@P2: In the case where n is even but not divisible by 4, we again define a numbering\nL128@P2: Yij of the pairs in Yi. However, we choose a somewhat non-intuitive ordering,\nL129@P2: for reasons that will be clear later. (This ordering is also reflected in the way\nL130@P2: we have arranged the pairs in Figure (3).) We define Yij for j from 1 to n−2\nL131@P2: 4\nL132@P2: to\nL133@P2: be the pair {i + j, i − j}. Then we let Yi\nL134@P2: n+2\nL135@P2: 4\nL136@P2: = {i, n}. Finally, for j from n+6\nL137@P2: 4\nL138@P2: to\nL139@P2: n\nL140@P2: 2\nL141@P2: , we let Yij = {i + j − 1, i − j + 1}. Intuitively, we start with the pairs in the\nL142@P2: “natural” order: {i, n}, {i + 1, i − 1}, etc. Then we move the pair {i, n} so it is\nL143@P2: the middle pair in our list. This creates the ordering just described.\nL144@P2: Notice we have arranged the edges Yij in order in our table, so we can restate\nL145@P2: our previous observation as saying that each vertex other than n appears twice\nL146@P2: in each column other than the middle column.\nL147@P2: There is one other property of the sets Yij which will be important for our\nL148@P2: construction below. In Figure 2, note that for any two vertices w and x, the\nL149@P2: number of rows for which w’s pair appears to the left of x’s is the same as the\nL150@P2: number of rows for which w’s pair appears to the right of x’s. For example, 3\nL151@P2: appears to the left of 6 in rows 2 and 4, and 3 appears to the right of 6 in rows\nL152@P2-3: 35 and 7. (Row 1 has the pair {3, 6}, and 3 and 6 are absent from rows 3 and\nL153@P3: 6,respectively, so these rows are ignored.) A similar statement is true about\nL154@P3: Figure 3. We formalize these observations in the following lemma.\nL155@P3: Lemma 2. If n is even and not a multiple of 4, let w and x be two vertices.\nL156@P3: For i from 1 to n, define wi and xi so that w ∈ Yiwi and x ∈ Yixi. Then wi < xi\nL157@P3: for exactly n\nL158@P3: 2 − 2 values of i.\nL159@P3: If n is odd, let w and x be any two vertices. For i from 1 to n, define wi and\nL160@P3: xi so that w ∈ Yiwi and x ∈ Yixi, assuming such values exist. Then wi < xi for\nL161@P3: exactly n−3\nL162@P3: 2\nL163@P3: values of i.\nL164@P3: Proof. We begin with the odd case. Imagine a circle with the n points\nL165@P3: 1, 2, . . . n drawn, in order, equally spaced around the circle. Then the pairs Yij\nL166@P3: are, in order from j = 1 to j = k, the two vertices closest to i, then the next\nL167@P3: two vertices, etc. (See Figure 4.)\nL168@P3: .\nL169@P3: .\nL170@P3: .\nL171@P3: ..\nL172@P3: ..\nL173@P3: ..\nL174@P3: ...\nL175@P3: ...\nL176@P3: ....\nL177@P3: ....\nL178@P3: ....\nL179@P3: ......\nL180@P3: ......\nL181@P3: ........\nL182@P3: .........\nL183@P3: ..........\nL184@P3: ................. ................... ........ .... ....... ....\nL185@P3: ...........\nL186@P3: ........\nL187@P3: ........\nL188@P3: .......\nL189@P3: ......\nL190@P3: .....\nL191@P3: ....\nL192@P3: ....\nL193@P3: ...\nL194@P3: ...\nL195@P3: ..\nL196@P3: ..\nL197@P3: ..\nL198@P3: ..\nL199@P3: .\nL200@P3: .\nL201@P3: .............................................................................................................................................................................................\nL202@P3: .\nL203@P3: • • 1\nL204@P3: •\nL205@P3: 2\nL206@P3: •\nL207@P3: 7\nL208@P3: .\nL209@P3: •\nL210@P3: 3\nL211@P3: •\nL212@P3: 6\nL213@P3: .\nL214@P3: •\nL215@P3: 4\nL216@P3: •\nL217@P3: 5\nL218@P3: .\nL219@P3: Figure 4: The set Y1 in the case n = 7\n"
